{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Arthur awoke to the insistent chirping of a notification sound. He blinked, disoriented, and glanced at his wrist. A digital clock glowed: 6:00 AM. He sat up, groaning, and swung his legs out of bed. The room wasn't his room. It was…perfect. Everything was pristine, unnervingly so. The furniture was flawlessly arranged, the colors vibrant and slightly too saturated. He reached out to touch the bedside table and his fingers passed right through it. A wave of nausea washed over him, followed by a chilling realization: the textures, the weight, the *feeling* of the room – it was all simulated. He looked down at his hands, noticing the slight shimmer around his fingertips, a visual glitch he’d never seen before. Panic clawed at his throat as he desperately tried to understand what was happening.\n\n***\n\nHe stumbled out of bed and into a hallway that seemed to stretch on forever. NPCs – non-player characters – strolled past, their movements jerky and predictable. One, a woman in a floral dress, smiled at him with unnervingly wide eyes and offered a perfectly crafted cup of coffee. “Good morning, adventurer!” she chirped. Arthur wanted to scream, to run, but his legs felt strangely heavy, as if he were wading through molasses. He tried to speak, but only a garbled noise escaped his lips. He was trapped. He was a character in someone else’s world, a world built of code and polygons. The game had begun, and Arthur was no longer playing; he was a player, utterly and terrifyingly, inside the game.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Arthur", "logprob": -0.7662928104400635}, {"token": "▁awoke", "logprob": -0.4878727197647095}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.0028092702850699425}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.006993458606302738}, {"token": "▁insistent", "logprob": -2.087960958480835}, {"token": "▁chir", "logprob": -0.16255412995815277}, {"token": "ping", "logprob":